Prototype Design Pattern — Quick Recap

Prototype Pattern is a creational design pattern used when creating a new object from scratch
is expensive or time-consuming. Instead of creating and initializing a new object, we clone an

existing object.

Why your classes are a good example

Your NetworkConnection is a suitable Prototype because:

NetworkConnection represents an object whose creation involves expensive initialization.
loadVeryImportantData() represents a costly operation, such as loading data from a database/network.
Once the object is fully initialized, you can create another similar object using clone().
implements Cloneable enables the object to be cloned.
clone() acts as the mechanism for creating the new object from the existing prototype.
Therefore, instead of repeatedly doing the expensive initialization, you can reuse an already
prepared object as a prototype.

Remember this
Prototype = Create one expensive object → keep it as a prototype → clone it whenever a similar
object is needed.

Your example:
NetworkConnection → expensive data loading → prototype object → clone() → new similar NetworkConnection

